  "cause": "On 1 November 2024, a concrete canopy collapsed outside the railway station in Novi Sad and killed 16 people. The renovation that produced it was a showcase project of a state governed since 2012 by Aleksandar Vučić's Serbian Progressive Party, and the country read the collapse the way it reads most such events — as the visible edge of a system. **Students blockaded streets, then held weekly protests, then built assemblies and plenums, and the movement spread outward into farmers, actors and judges until it was no longer a student story: in January 2025 the prime minister resigned \"to defuse tensions\", and in March 2025 Belgrade saw the largest anti-government protest in Serbia's history** (BBC). On Sunday, in snap elections Vučić called himself last month, the movement's \"Student List — Students Win\" took **52.9% of the vote on a turnout of 69.34%, the highest in years, against 2.2 million votes for the students and 1.72 million for the SNS; the projection by CeSID and Ipsos gives the list 140 of 250 parliamentary seats** (BBC, Reuters). **\"It is clear that we have lost these elections,\" Vučić said at SNS headquarters — conceding on the night, unusually early — and then, in the same speech, alleged without evidence that the students had the support of unnamed \"European countries and the American deep state\"** (BBC). **The list fielded candidates but no students.** It ran doctors, professors, an opera singer, a former basketball star, an actor and a former central-bank governor across all 250 seats — a deliberate calculation that an electorate would not hand the state to people with no administrative experience. **Its lead candidate, the cardiologist Ilija Srdanović, told the BBC: \"We offer our citizens the core values of civilised society, which this previous regime has taken away from our citizens for the last time.\" The manifesto is specific: purge officials who broke the rules and cracked down on protesters, stop party loyalists from running public enterprises, free the public broadcaster of political control. In Belgrade, the metal fences of \"Ćaciland\" — the pro-Vučić tent camp pitched 18 months ago in Pioneers Park in front of parliament — were torn down to chants of \"Vučić is leaving\"** (BBC). European Commissioner for Enlargement Marta Kos said \"a new chapter can begin for Serbia. The door to the EU is open.\" Hungary's Péter Magyar, whose party ended sixteen years of Orbán rule in April, said he was delighted to hear from the Student List's leaders \"how much strength our victory in April had given them.\"",
