  "cause": "Israel votes on Tuesday in an election its own press is calling existential, and the campaign has narrowed to two men and two answers. **Polls published on Friday — the last permitted under Israeli election law — put Benjamin Netanyahu's governing coalition and the main opposition bloc almost neck and neck, with some 500,000 first-time voters eligible and reportedly 70,000 Israelis flying in from abroad specifically to vote** (BBC). The opposition's face is **Gadi Eisenkot**, a former military chief of staff, relatively new to politics, of Moroccan working-class background, whose son was killed in the Gaza war, and who has campaigned on a state commission of inquiry into the 7 October 2023 attacks — a measure a recent survey suggests more than 70% of Jewish Israelis support — and on enforcing court rulings that draft ultra-Orthodox Jews into military service. **Netanyahu's campaign is built on security and the slogan \"No Surrender\", casting his rivals as soft on it** (BBC). The campaign has already produced its own data point: a pro-Netanyahu speaker referred to Eisenkot as a Moroccan \"monkey\" on television. **Two days before the vote, the state closed the territory it governs.** After two Palestinian gunmen killed two Israeli soldiers at a checkpoint on Friday, **Defence Minister Israel Katz announced an operation in Hebron in which about 2,000 residents have been displaced and, according to footage shared online, at least one whole street appears flattened; the city's 200,000 residents had been cordoned before the operation was even named. The IDF then announced that all West Bank land crossings would be closed on election day and that Palestinian workers would be barred from all settlements and industrial zones** (BBC). Before 7 October 2023, more than 100,000 Palestinian workers held permits into Israel; since then only a small fraction have been restored. **Hebron's mayor, Youssef al-Jaabari, said the operation was \"driven by the elections\" and that Netanyahu and his ministers were trying to secure the votes of the far right and the settlers. Netanyahu told Israel's i24 channel on Saturday that the Palestinian Authority should expect a \"very big surprise\"** (BBC). Separately, the Times of Israel reported what it called an \"unprecedented confrontation\" in which the IDF chief of staff resisted a Netanyahu order for military action tied to Iran; the defence minister has said Israel is not planning an Iran attack before the election (Times of Israel, Yahoo).",
